oanoke.edu/majors/engineering). We seek a candidate with energetic vision, creative thought, and collaborative spirit. A Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering or a closely-related field is required. A successful candidate must have a strong interest in teaching a wide range of undergraduate science and engineering-related courses in a liberal arts setting, in mentoring a variety of experiential learning opportunities, and in supporting efforts towards ABET accreditation. Evidence of potential for highly effective classroom teaching is required. The likely teaching load includes courses in Circuits & Electronics, Instrumentation & Control Systems Engineering, General Education, and Introductory Physics Laboratory, with potential to develop new courses. More information about Roanoke College’s general education program can be found at https://www.roanoke.edu/inside/intellectual_inquiry This position is part of a cluster of tenure-track hires within the School of Health, Science, and Sustainability, including Engineering, Environmental Studies, Health and Exercise Science, Psychology, and Neuroscience. Consistent with Roanoke College's commitment to transdisciplinary education, this hire is envisioned as contributing to an interconnected academic ecosystem rather than a single program. Faculty hired through this initiative will be expected to intentionally cultivate meaningful collaborations across disciplines through their teaching, scholarship, and service. Applicants should address in their cover letter how they would contribute to this transdisciplinary vision with at least one specific opportunity for collaboration across two or more programs at Roanoke College. To learn more about these programs and others within the School of Health, Science, and Sustainability, visit https://www.roanoke.edu/academics/schools. The selected candidate for this position must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire. The College is unable to provide any type of visa sponsorships for this position at this time. To apply, please submit the following application materials: •a cover letter •curriculum vitae •teaching statement •research statement •unofficial copies of graduate and undergraduate transcripts Review of applications will begin December 14, 2026. All completed applications received by that date will be given full consideration, and applications received after the deadline may still be considered. After the initial review of applicants, three reference letters will be requested (at least two must directly address teaching). Questions about the position may be sent, through email, to the chair of the search Committee, Assistant Dean of the School of Health, Science, and Sustainability, Dr. Richard Keithley Position Summary: Tenure-track faculty members serve multiple roles as teachers, mentors, colleagues, and scholars. Full-time faculty are expected not only to excel in classroom instruction but also to fulfill various responsibilities as active members of the college community. Key Responsibilities (Areas): Teaching •Teach a standard full-time course load as defined by the College (may be a mix of undergraduate and graduate courses) •Develop and deliver high-quality courses in the faculty member’s area(s) of expertise •Use inclusive, effective, engaging, and current pedagogical practices •Fairly assess student learning and provide timely feedback •Assign grades and maintain course/student records in accordance with FERPA and college regulations and submit grades and records by established deadlines. •Hold regular office hours to provide student support Scholarly and Creative Activity Maintain an active program of scholarly, creative, or professional work appropriate to the discipline and/or the scholarship of teaching and learning •Demonstrate ongoing intellectual engagement that contributes to the field and/or enhances teaching and advising; remain current •Share scholarly and creative work through appropriate 