ertise in the environmental sciences with an emphasis on applying physical science to understand and address environmental challenges at the Earth’s surface. Relevant areas of expertise include hydrology and watershed science, environmental geology, environmental chemistry, Earth systems science, physical geography, climate science and earth-atmosphere systems, or related fields. The ability to teach introductory and upper-level environmental science courses is required; preference will be given to candidates who can also teach courses in GIS or spatial analysis. Candidates must demonstrate the potential for excellence in undergraduate teaching and mentorship along with the ability to collaborate effectively as part of an interdisciplinary program. We value undergraduate teaching experience, demonstrated interest in experiential and active learning approaches, and research or practitioner experience that incorporates community engagement. A strong record of scholarship and development of a research program that includes collaboration with undergraduate students is expected. This position is part of a cluster of tenure-track hires within the School of Health, Science, and Sustainability, including Engineering, Environmental Studies, Health and Exercise Science, Psychology, and Neuroscience. Consistent with Roanoke College's commitment to transdisciplinary education, this hire is envisioned as contributing to an interconnected academic ecosystem rather than a single program. Faculty hired through this initiative will be expected to intentionally cultivate meaningful collaborations across disciplines through their teaching, scholarship, and service. Applicants should address in their cover letter how they would contribute to this transdisciplinary vision with at least one specific opportunity for collaboration across two or more programs at Roanoke College. To learn more about these programs and others within the School of Health, Science, and Sustainability, visit https://www.roanoke.edu/academics/schools . The salary range for this position is 65,000-70,000, commensurate with experience. The selected candidate for this position must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire. The College is unable to provide any type of visa sponsorships for this position at this time. Key Responsibilities: Teach a standard full-time course load as defined by the College. The likely teaching load is four courses per semester and includes introductory environmental science, upper-level courses in area of specialty, and general education courses. Develop and deliver high-quality courses in the faculty member’s area(s) of expertise Use inclusive, effective, engaging, and current pedagogical practices Fairly assess student learning and provide timely feedback Assign grades and maintain course/student records in accordance with FERPA and college regulations and submit grades and records by established deadlines. Hold regular office hours to provide student support Education, Experience, Skills, and Abilities: Ph.D. in Environmental Science or a closely related field is preferred Master’s Degree or higher with relevant professional experience is required Evidence of highly effective classroom teaching or potential of such is required A successful candidate must have a strong interest in teaching a wide range of undergraduate courses in a liberal arts setting, some of which include dual enrollment students, and collaborating with colleagues on pedagogical innovations To apply, please submit the following application materials: a cover letter curriculum vitae teaching statement research statement unofficial copies of graduate and undergraduate transcripts Review of applications will begin November 6, 2026 . All completed applications received by that date will be given full consideration, and applications received after the deadline may still be considered. After the initial review of applicants, three reference letters will be requested (at least two must directly address teaching). Questions about the position may be sent, through email, to the chair of the search Committee, Assistant Dean of the School of Health, Science, and Sustainability, Chris Lassiter, lassiter@ roanoke.edu Position Summary: Tenure-track faculty members serve multiple roles as teachers, mentors, colleagues, and scholars. Full-time faculty are expected not only to excel in classroom instruction but also to fulfill various responsibilities as active members of the college community. Key Responsibilities (Areas) : Teaching Teach a standard full-time course load as defined by the College (may be a mix of undergraduate and graduate courses) Develop and deliver high-quality courses in the faculty member’s area(s) of expertise Use inclusive, effective, engaging, and current pedagogical practices Fairly assess student learning and provide timely feedback Assign grades and maintain course/student records in accordance with FERPA and college regulations and submit grades and records by established deadlines. Hold regular office hours to provide student support Scholarly and Creative Activity Maintain an active program of scholarly, creative, or professional work appropriate to the discipline and/or the scholarship of teaching and learning Demonstrate ongoing intellectual engagement that contributes to the field and/or enhances teaching and advising; remain current Share scholarly and creative work through appropriate venues (e.g., publications, presentations, exhibitions) Consistently participate in professional development opportunities to enhance teaching, curriculum development, and scholarship Service Participate in faculty governance through committee membership, etc. Participate in program, School, and/or College-wide service Contribute to curriculum development and program assessment Attend program, School, and College-wide meetings, and academic events such as Convocation and Commencement (a professional responsibility) Mentor colleagues Education, Experience, Skills, and Abilities : Required Qualifications For tenure-track faculty, terminal degree in the discipline (or closely related field) Demonstrated potential for effective undergraduate teaching For tenure-track faculty, demonstrated potential for scholarly or creative activity Preferred Qualifications College-level teaching experience, with evidence of effective and inclusive teaching practices Interest in advising and mentoring students and contributing to a campus community Good written and verbal communication skills Good organizational skills Good critical thinking and problem-solving skills Good interpersonal and collaborative skills Return to Roanoke College Apply for this job Apply for this job Apply for this job SHARE Not the right job? Join our Talent Network Location Return to Roanoke College POWERED BY APPLICANT TRACKING"
