purpose-driven experiential learning, high-quality research and professional experiences, and individualized student mentorship and advising. The Health and Exercise Science program is well-connected to regional healthcare systems, biomedical research facilities, and public and nonprofit health and wellness organizations. Housed in the Cregger Center, students and faculty share state-of-the-art classrooms, research facilities and equipment. The successful candidate will teach high-quality undergraduate courses, provide thoughtful academic advising, mentor students in undergraduate research and/or professional experiences, maintain an active line of independent research, and serve the department, college, and community. Teaching responsibilities will include both core and elective courses and laboratories within the Health and Exercise Science curriculum, with opportunities to develop new courses according to the candidate’s expertise, as well as contributions to our general education curriculum. More information about Roanoke College’s general education program can be found at https://www.roanoke.edu/inside/intellectual_inquiry The program is particularly interested in individuals with interest and expertise in strength & conditioning teaching and research. Teaching load is eight teaching units per year, where lab instruction and advising contribute toward teaching units. Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate in Exercise Science, Kinesiology or related field; Demonstrated evidence of the potential for high-quality teaching; Demonstrated evidence of the potential for an active research agenda; Demonstrated commitment to the values and mission of a liberal arts education; Preferred Qualifications: Expertise and teaching experience in Strength and Conditioning Capacity to support the teaching of Human Biology and Human Anatomy & Physiology NSCA CSCS certification or similar This position is part of a cluster of tenure-track hires within the School of Health, Science, and Sustainability, including Engineering, Environmental Studies, Health and Exercise Science, Psychology, and Neuroscience. Consistent with Roanoke College's commitment to transdisciplinary education, this hire is envisioned as contributing to an interconnected academic ecosystem rather than a single program. Faculty hired through this initiative will be expected to intentionally cultivate meaningful collaborations across disciplines through their teaching, scholarship, and service. Applicants should address in their cover letter how they would contribute to this transdisciplinary vision with at least one specific opportunity for collaboration across two or more programs at Roanoke College. To learn more about these programs and others within the School of Health, Science, and Sustainability, visit https://www.roanoke.edu/academics/schools. The selected candidate for this position must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire. The College is unable to provide any type of visa sponsorships for this position at this time. To apply, please submit the following application materials: a cover letter curriculum vitae teaching statement research statement unofficial copies of graduate and undergraduate transcripts Review of applications will begin December 1st, 2026. All completed applications received by that date will be given full consideration, and applications received after the deadline may still be considered. After the initial review of applicants, three reference letters will be requested (at least two must directly address teaching). Questions about the position may be sent, through email, to the chair of the search Committee, Assistant Dean of the School of Health, Science, and Sustainability, Dr. David Nichols . Position Summary: Tenure-track faculty members serve multiple roles as teachers, mentors, colleagues, and scholars. Full-time faculty are expected not only to excel in classroom instruction but also to fulfill various responsibilities as active members of the college community. Key Responsibilities (Areas): Teaching Teach a standard full-time course load as defined by the College (may be a mix of undergraduate and graduate courses) Develop and deliver high-quality courses in the faculty member’s area(s) of expertise Use inclusive, effective, engaging, and current pedagogical practices Fairly assess student learning and provide timely feedback Assign grades and maintain course/student records in accordance with FERPA and college regulations and submit grades and records by established deadlines. Hold regular office hours to provide student support Scholarly and Creative Activity Maintain an active program of scholarly, creative, or professional work appropriate to the discipline and/or the scholarship of teaching and learning Demonstrate ongoing intellectual engagement that contributes to the field and/or enhances teaching and advising; remain current Share scholarly and creative work through appropriate venues (e.g., publications, presentations, exhibitions) Consistently participate in professional development opportunities to enhance teaching, curriculum development, and scholarship Service Participate in faculty governance through committee membership, etc. Participate in program, School, and/or College-wide service Contribute to curriculum development and program assessment Attend program, School, and College-wide meetings, and academic events such as Convocation and Commencement (a professional responsibility) Mentor colleagues Education, Experience, Skills, and Abilities: Required Qualifications For tenure-track faculty, terminal degree in the discipline (or closely related field) Demonstrated potential for effective undergraduate teaching For tenure-track faculty, demonstrated potential for scholarly or creative activity Preferred Qualifications College-level teaching experience, with evidence of effective and inclusive teaching practices Interest in advising and mentoring students and contributing to a campus community Good written and verbal communication skills Good organizational skills Good critical thinking and problem-solving skills Good interpersonal and collaborative skills Return to Roanoke College Apply for this job Apply for this job Apply for this job SHARE Not the right job? Join our Talent Network Location Return to Roanoke College POWERED BY APPLICANT TRACKING"
    }
  },
